response:
The car’s initial kinetic energy is the energy it has because of its motion:

\[
\text{KE}_{\text{initial}} = \frac12 m v^{2}
          = \frac12 (1000\ \text{kg})(20\ \text{m/s})^{2}
          = \frac12 (1000)(400)
          = 200\,000\ \text{J}.
\]

The brakes convert part of this kinetic energy into heat (or other forms) that stay inside the braking system. They are said to “absorb” 180 000 J.

The total energy must be conserved, so whatever kinetic energy is **not** taken up by the brakes must go elsewhere—usually into the surrounding air, road, and sound. Thus

\[
\text{Energy lost to environment}
   = \text{KE}_{\text{initial}} - \text{energy absorbed by brakes}
   = 200\,000\ \text{J} - 180\,000\ \text{J}
   = 20\,000\ \text{J}.
\]

So 20 000 joules (or 20 kJ) of the car’s original kinetic energy are transferred to the environment.

Both laws have the same mathematical form: the force is **inversely proportional to the square of the distance** between the objects.

\[
\text{Newton’s gravitation:}\qquad 
F_{\text g}= \frac{G\,m_1m_2}{r^{2}}
\]

\[
\text{Coulomb’s law:}\qquad 
F_{\text e}= \frac{k\,q_1q_2}{r^{2}}
\]

- \(G\) is the gravitational constant, \(k\) (often written \(1/4\pi\varepsilon_0\)) is Coulomb’s constant.  
- \(m_1, m_2\) are the masses; \(q_1, q_2\) are the electric charges.  
- \(r\) is the separation between the two objects.

Now suppose the distance is **doubled**, i.e. \(r \rightarrow 2r\).

\[
F' = \frac{G\,m_1m_2}{(2r)^{2}} 
   = \frac{G\,m_1m_2}{4r^{2}} 
   = \frac{1}{4}\,F_{\text g}
\]

\[
F' = \frac{k\,q_1q_2}{(2r)^{2}} 
   = \frac{k\,q_1q_2}{4r^{2}} 
   = \frac{1}{4}\,F_{\text e}
\]

**Result:** When the separation between the two objects is doubled, the force in both Newton’s law of gravitation and Coulomb’s law becomes **one‑fourth (¼)** of its original value. The force is still attractive (gravity) or attractive/repulsive (electric), but its magnitude is reduced to 25 % of what it was.
